<%#
  The Vesopa lockup, in one place.

  There were eleven copies of this <img> across the views, each with its own
  width, its own height and its own guess at which of the two PNGs suited the
  background it sat on. Three of them were wrong: the black wordmark on a dark
  card renders as a black rectangle you cannot see, which is exactly the "logo
  not showing" people reported. One partial, one decision.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE WORDMARK FILE IS THE WHOLE LOGO. IT ALREADY CONTAINS THE V.
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  vesopa_logo.png is not the word "esopa" waiting for a mark to be placed in
  front of it — it is the finished lockup, green slash and all. This partial
  used to draw the inline SVG V *and then* that image, so every full lockup in
  the panel, the rail, the emails and the checkout rendered as

      V  Vesopa

  with the V doubled. It reads as a rendering fault rather than a logo, and it
  was on every signed-in page in the product.

  So the two modes are now genuinely exclusive:

    full lockup   the PNG alone. It is the logo. Nothing goes beside it.
    mark          the inline SVG V alone, for a collapsed rail or a favicon-
                  sized slot where 130px of wordmark cannot fit.

  Never both. If a space is too small for the wordmark, it wants `mark: true`;
  it does not want the wordmark with a second V bolted on.

  THE MARK IS INLINE SVG AND THE WORDMARK IS A PNG, on purpose. The V is
  geometry — crisp at 18px and at 180px, costs no request, and can be
  recoloured for whatever it sits on. The wordmark is set type, and tracing type
  by hand produces a logo that is nearly right, which is worse than a bitmap
  that is exactly right.

  Options:
    on      'light' (default) — for white and off-white backgrounds
            'dark'            — for the near-black chrome
    size    height of the lockup in px (default 22)
    mark    true to render the V alone, no wordmark — for a collapsed rail,
            where a 130px-wide image was being cropped with object-fit
    href    wrap it in a link to here (omit for a bare image)
%>
<%
  const bOn = typeof on !== 'undefined' ? on : 'light';
  const bSize = typeof size !== 'undefined' ? size : 22;
  const bMarkOnly = typeof mark !== 'undefined' && mark;
  const bHref = typeof href !== 'undefined' ? href : null;
  // The V's own aspect ratio, straight off the viewBox in mark.svg.
  const bMarkW = Math.round((bSize * 100) / 70);
  const bInk = bOn === 'dark' ? '#ffffff' : '#111111';
%>
<% if (bHref) { %><a class="brand-lockup" href="<%= bHref %>" aria-label="Vesopa"><% } else { %><span class="brand-lockup"><% } %>
  <% if (bMarkOnly) { %>
    <%# The attributes are written out literally rather than through an escaping
        interpolation. Building them as a string and printing it escaped is what
        produced the invalid aria-hidden="&quot;true&quot;" that shipped on
        every rail in the panel. %>
    <svg class="brand-v" width="<%= bMarkW %>" height="<%= bSize %>" viewBox="0 0 100 70"
         role="img" aria-label="Vesopa">
      <path fill="#a5c715" d="M0,0 L22.7,0 L50.7,49 L77.3,0 L100,0 L62,70 L40,70 Z"/>
      <path fill="<%= bInk %>" d="M58.3,35 L81,35 L62,70 L40,70 L50.7,49 Z"/>
    </svg>
  <% } else { %>
    <%# `height` is set and `width` deliberately is not: the intrinsic ratio of
        the file decides the width, so a new wordmark cannot squash itself. %>
    <img class="brand-word"
         src="/assets/img/brand/vesopa_logo<%= bOn === 'dark' ? '_on_dark' : '' %>.png"
         alt="Vesopa" height="<%= bSize %>" style="height:<%= bSize %>px;width:auto">
  <% } %>
<% if (bHref) { %></a><% } else { %></span><% } %>
